    {
      "id": "CGWCMnO0rQMr3Ddxp7QB6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3719.368997106781,
      "y": 8801.692769827054,
      "width": 1429.5113525390625,
      "height": 245,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4G",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 247493992,
      "version": 82,
      "versionNonce": 1954350872,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782976112256,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2) User should be able to send/receive message\n\nTo send a message:\n- User sends a sendMessage message to the API Server.\n- The API Server looks up all participants in the chat via the Membership table.\n- The API Server looks up the websocket connection for each participant in its internal hash table and \nsends the message via each connection.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2) User should be able to send/receive message\n\nTo send a message:\n- User sends a sendMessage message to the API Server.\n- The API Server looks up all participants in the chat via the Membership table.\n- The API Server looks up the websocket connection for each participant in its internal hash table and \nsends the message via each connection.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "_XlWI0eIrYETLzxyVfLpN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1606.4372611042877,
      "y": 6276.632339341468,
      "width": 2268.558349609375,
      "height": 1400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4I",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1435051544,
      "version": 218,
      "versionNonce": 83964440,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782977158926,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "WebSocket — Connection & Fan-out Notes\nInitial connection / metadata registration\n\nWhen a user connects to a WS, it sends metadata to the server — specifically, which channels it is part of.\nExample: WS1 has connections from A, B, C, D.\n\nA → C1 (A is part of channel C1)\nB → C1\nC → C1\nD → C1\n\n\nWS1 holds this metadata (membership details) locally.\n\nNaive inter-server routing (v1)\n\nWS1 connects to WS2 using a TCP connection.\nWhen A sends a message: it's sent to B, C, D (local, same server) — then forwarded over TCP to WS2, which sends it to E (also in channel C1).\nProblem: multiple TCP connections to different WebSocket servers leads to connection wastage — those connections could otherwise be used to connect more users \nand deliver messages to them.\n\nPub/Sub fix (v2)\nIntroduce Redis Pub/Sub between WS1, WS2, WS3, WS4.\nThis solves the TCP overhead problem (no more all-to-all TCP mesh between edge servers).\n\nRedis Pub/Sub:\nDoesn't persist messages.\nSends messages in real-time to connected (subscribed) servers only.\n\n\n\nRemaining problem with naive pub/sub\n\nIf WS1 just publishes to pub/sub and pub/sub pushes the message to all of WS2, WS3, WS4 — that's wasteful when WS3 and WS4 have no users in channel C1.\nFix: Redis Pub/Sub needs a mapping of subscriptions — e.g. WS1 → subscribes to C1 — established when WS1 connects to pub/sub for the first time \n(registers interest in only the channels its connected users care about).\n\nEdge Servers\nBecause WebSockets are expensive and persistent (TCP), and browsers have a 6-concurrent-TCP-connection limit, we have to \nmultiplex all real-time communication over one WebSocket connection — this is the reasoning for edge servers.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "WebSocket — Connection & Fan-out Notes\nInitial connection / metadata registration\n\nWhen a user connects to a WS, it sends metadata to the server — specifically, which channels it is part of.\nExample: WS1 has connections from A, B, C, D.\n\nA → C1 (A is part of channel C1)\nB → C1\nC → C1\nD → C1\n\n\nWS1 holds this metadata (membership details) locally.\n\nNaive inter-server routing (v1)\n\nWS1 connects to WS2 using a TCP connection.\nWhen A sends a message: it's sent to B, C, D (local, same server) — then forwarded over TCP to WS2, which sends it to E (also in channel C1).\nProblem: multiple TCP connections to different WebSocket servers leads to connection wastage — those connections could otherwise be used to connect more users \nand deliver messages to them.\n\nPub/Sub fix (v2)\nIntroduce Redis Pub/Sub between WS1, WS2, WS3, WS4.\nThis solves the TCP overhead problem (no more all-to-all TCP mesh between edge servers).\n\nRedis Pub/Sub:\nDoesn't persist messages.\nSends messages in real-time to connected (subscribed) servers only.\n\n\n\nRemaining problem with naive pub/sub\n\nIf WS1 just publishes to pub/sub and pub/sub pushes the message to all of WS2, WS3, WS4 — that's wasteful when WS3 and WS4 have no users in channel C1.\nFix: Redis Pub/Sub needs a mapping of subscriptions — e.g. WS1 → subscribes to C1 — established when WS1 connects to pub/sub for the first time \n(registers interest in only the channels its connected users care about).\n\nEdge Servers\nBecause WebSockets are expensive and persistent (TCP), and browsers have a 6-concurrent-TCP-connection limit, we have to \nmultiplex all real-time communication over one WebSocket connection — this is the reasoning for edge servers.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
